<p class="sst-lead">Apply a <strong>rigid motion</strong> (rotate / reflect / translate). The shape moves on the canvas,
but the PRRTI invariants &mdash; the singular values \((\sigma_1,\sigma_2)\) <em>and</em> the eigenfunctions
\(\widetilde{v}_j(s)\), the columns of \(\widetilde{V}\) &mdash; <strong>do not budge</strong>.</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>\[ k_{\mathcal{T}}[R\bm{c}+\bm{b}]=\big(R\,\mathcal{T}[\bm{c}]\big)\cdot\big(R\,\mathcal{T}[\bm{c}]\big)
  =\mathcal{T}[\bm{c}]\cdot\big(R^{\top}R\,\mathcal{T}[\bm{c}]\big)=k_{\mathcal{T}}[\bm{c}]. \]</p>
  <p>The right panel plots the eigenfunctions \(\widetilde{v}_j(s)=\tfrac{1}{\sigma_j}\,\bm{\alpha}_j\cdot\mathcal{T}[\bm{c}](s)\).
  Under a rigid motion they hold still &mdash; <strong>by uniqueness of the polar decomposition</strong>
  \(X=\widetilde{X}P\), the rotation is absorbed into the principal directions \(\bm{\alpha}_j\) (drawn on the
  curve), leaving the undulation class \([\widetilde{X}]\in\mathrm{Gr}(2,n)\) fixed.</p>
  <p class="sst-note">Equivalently one may fix canonical \(\bm{\alpha}_j=\bm{e}_j\) by hand; then a rotation
  <em>phase-shifts</em> \(\widetilde{v}_j(s)\mapsto\widetilde{v}_j(s+\theta)\) instead &mdash; the same equivalence
  class, a different chart.</p>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>The one freedom no convention removes is the <strong>cyclic reparametrization</strong>: press
  <strong>permute</strong> and the eigenfunctions genuinely phase-shift. That ambiguity is settled later by
  <strong>cyclic Procrustes</strong> alignment, not here.</p>
  <p class="sst-note">Drag a landmark and the eigenfunctions <em>do</em> change &mdash; that is genuine
  <strong>undulation</strong>, the shape content SST is built to measure.</p>
</div>
